course: everyday
lesson: 80
title: Sleep and healthy eating
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Sleep and food are the body's fuel and repair: while you sleep the body grows and fixes itself and the brain sorts what you learned, and food gives the body what it needs to work.
explain: Good sleep comes from a calm evening: screens away about an hour before bed, no caffeine, a dark, quiet, cool bedroom and wake-up times that are similar every day.
explain: A good plate is about half vegetables and fruit, a quarter grains like bread or rice, and a quarter protein like meat, fish, beans or eggs, because different foods give different things.
explain: Food can also make you ill: germs grow fast in warm food left out, in spoiled food, and in raw eggs, flour and meat, so food is washed, kept cool and cooked properly.
explain: A girl in Arizona does not like broccoli at first, but she tries a small bite at a few dinners, and after a while she finds she likes it with cheese.
fact: The light and excitement of screens make it harder to fall asleep, so screens are put away about an hour before bed.
fact: Lying quietly, breathing slowly or reading a calm book helps the body relax when you cannot sleep.
fact: Caffeine in cola and energy drinks keeps you awake, and energy drinks are not for children.
fact: Similar wake-up times every day give the body a steady rhythm.
fact: A dark, quiet and cool bedroom helps deep sleep.
fact: Sleepwalking and sleep-talking are common in children and usually harmless, and a parent can keep you safe at night.
fact: Half the plate is vegetables and fruit, a quarter grains and a quarter protein.
fact: Tastes change, so trying a small bite of a new food several times helps you learn to like it.
fact: Eating while running or lying down can make you choke, so you sit down to eat.
fact: It is all right to stop eating when you are full and say politely that you have had enough.
fact: Crisps and sweets have little goodness and lots of salt, sugar and fat, so treats are kept small and occasional.
fact: Fruit and vegetables are washed before eating to remove dirt and chemicals.
fact: Spoiled food, food past its use-by date and food left out for hours on a hot day can cause food poisoning.
fact: Raw eggs, raw flour and uncooked meat can carry germs, so they are eaten only when properly cooked.
exercise: Why should screens be put away about an hour before bed? | Their light and excitement make it harder to fall asleep | Screens get too hot at night | It saves the battery so the screen is fully charged for the morning | Screens only work in the daytime
exercise: You cannot fall asleep. What helps? | Lie quietly, breathe slowly or read a calm book | Play a fast video game until you feel very tired out | Eat a big snack in bed | Jump around the room to tire yourself
exercise: Why should children avoid cola and energy drinks in the evening? | Caffeine keeps you awake | They make you sleep too deeply | They are too cold before bed | They make your dreams scary
exercise: You like to sleep until noon at weekends. What is better? | Keep wake-up times fairly similar each day | Sleep even longer on Sundays to catch up on the whole week | Stay up all Friday night instead | Sleep only at weekends
exercise: What kind of bedroom helps deep sleep? | Dark, quiet and cool | Bright, with the TV on | Very hot, with heavy blankets | Noisy, with music playing
exercise: Your sister walks around in her sleep. What should you do? | Tell a parent, who can keep her safe at night | Shout as loudly as you can to wake her up at once | Lock her in her room alone | Laugh and leave her walking
exercise: How should a healthy dinner plate be filled? | Half vegetables and fruit, a quarter grains, a quarter protein | Half meat and half chips, with a spoonful of peas on the side if you like | Mostly bread with a little fruit | All vegetables and nothing else
exercise: You do not want to try a new vegetable. What is a good idea? | Try a small bite, maybe several times | Never try it, first tastes never change | Hide it under the table | Eat only foods you already know
exercise: Why should you sit down to eat? | You can choke if you run or lie while eating | Food always tastes much better when you sit down | Standing makes you hungrier | It is only a rule for restaurants
exercise: You are full, but there is food left on your plate. What can you do? | Stop and say politely that you have had enough | Force yourself to eat it all, every last bite | Throw it on the floor | Hide it in your pocket
exercise: A friend eats only crisps and sweets for lunch. Why is that unhealthy? | They have little goodness and lots of salt, sugar and fat | They have far too many vitamins for a child to take in one meal | They are too heavy to digest | They make you grow too fast
exercise: You bought apples at the shop. What should you do before eating them? | Wash them | Peel them with your teeth | Put them in the freezer | Nothing, shop fruit is always clean
exercise: The yoghurt is past its use-by date and smells bad. What should you do? | Not eat it and tell an adult | Eat it quickly before it gets worse | Taste a little to check | Stir it and then eat it
exercise: A plate of chicken was left out of the fridge for hours on a hot day. Is it safe? | No, germs grow fast in warm food | Yes, if it still looks and smells fine | Yes, if you eat it cold | Yes, if you add ketchup
exercise: Why should you not eat raw cookie dough? | Raw eggs and flour can carry germs | It is too sweet for your teeth only | It sticks to the inside of your stomach | Uncooked dough has no taste
test: A boy in Atlanta plays on his tablet in bed until late and cannot sleep. What is the best change? | Put the tablet away an hour before bed and keep it out of the bedroom | Turn the brightness right up so his eyes get tired out more quickly | Play quieter games in bed | Drink cola to stay calm
test: Why is a cool, dark room good for sleep? | Good conditions help the body sleep deeply | Light makes dreams longer | Warm rooms make you grow too fast | Dark rooms keep all the monsters away
test: Which is true about trying new foods? | Many foods you dislike at first become liked after a few tries | If you dislike a food once, you will always dislike it all your life | Children's tastes never change | Only sweet foods can become liked
test: What would probably happen if a child ate a sandwich while running in the playground? | He could choke on a piece of food | He would digest it faster | He would run faster after eating | Nothing, running helps swallowing
test: A family picnic in Queensland: the potato salad has sat in the sun all afternoon. What is wise? | Do not eat it, it could cause food poisoning | Eat it, salads cannot go bad | Eat it if it still tastes nice | Put it in the shade for a bit and then eat it all at once
test: A girl drinks an energy drink before bed. Why will she find it hard to sleep? | The caffeine keeps her awake | The sugar makes her too sleepy | The bubbles tickle her stomach | The drink is too cold
test: A boy sleeps till midday on Saturday and Sunday and then cannot wake up on Monday. Why? | His body's rhythm was thrown off | He did not sleep long enough at the weekend | Mondays are always harder for everyone | His alarm clock was too quiet
test: Why is it fine to stop when you are full? | Listening to your body keeps it healthy | Leftover food is always bad | Eating less at every meal makes you grow taller | Your plate must always stay half full
test: A girl wants to taste the raw burger meat before her dad cooks it. Why not? | Uncooked meat can carry germs that make you ill | Raw meat has no taste at all | Raw meat is too hard to chew | Cooking only makes it look nicer on the plate, nothing more
test: Which plate is closest to a healthy dinner? | Vegetables and salad, some rice, and a piece of fish | A big plate of chips and a sausage | A bowl of sweets and a glass of cola | Only bread with butter
test: can i play on the ipad in bed till i fall asleep | Put screens away about an hour before bed | Yes, screens help you get sleepy faster | Yes, if the sound is off | Yes, games make you tired faster
test: can i have a can of energy drink for the match | No, energy drinks are not for children | Yes, they give you healthy energy for running about | Yes, if you only drink half | Yes, if it is a sugar free one
test: im full but theres still loads on my plate, do i hafta finish | No, you can stop when full and say so politely | Yes, you must always eat it all | Yes, or you will get really hungry later on tonight | Yes, leaving food is very rude
test: can i lick the bowl, its raw cookie dough with egg | No, raw eggs and flour can carry germs | Yes, raw dough is always safe to eat | Yes, if the eggs are fresh from the shop today | Yes, if you just have a little
explain: A boy in California played games until 2 a.m. most nights; at school he fell asleep in class, forgot his homework and snapped at his friends.
explain: A girl in Sydney drank fizzy drinks instead of water every day; she had fillings in four teeth before she was ten.
fact: Children of 9–10 need about 9 to 12 hours of sleep, and too little makes it harder to learn, remember and stay calm.
fact: Breakfast gives the body and brain fuel for the morning; skipping it can leave you wobbly and unable to focus.
fact: Sugary drinks are one of the biggest causes of tooth decay and extra weight in children.
test: stayed up till 2 on my game, tired af, whatever | Too little sleep makes you forget, get grumpy and learn worse | Sleep doesn't matter until you're grown up and go out to work every day | Staying up late makes you stronger | One late night every day is fine
test: why's mum so strict about fizzy drinks all the time | Lots of sugar rots teeth and is bad for your body | Fizzy drinks have no sugar | They only matter if you drink ten cans or more in a day | Bubbles clean your teeth
test: skipped brekkie again, feel all wobbly n cant focus | Eat something healthy, the body and brain need fuel | Skip lunch too to get used to it | Wobbly means you are getting fit | Have three energy drinks instead
test: did u hear a boy gamed every night till 3 and fell asleep in class and smashed his face on the desk, what's the real lesson | Kids need 9 to 12 hours of sleep to learn and stay well | Gaming is only bad on school nights | Sleep does not matter if you drink cola | Desks are dangerous and should be soft
test: my mate says eating raw cookie dough makes u sick instantly every time, true? | Exaggerated; raw flour and eggs can carry germs, so it is risky | True, you are sick the second you eat it | False, raw dough is perfectly safe | True, but only if the dough is cold
test: everyone's saying Josh is fat cos he eats crisps all day, should i pass it on | No, it's unkind to talk about people's bodies | Yes, everyone should know his diet | Yes, but only to the dinner ladies | Yes, and tell him to stop eating
exam: Identify the principal reason for which screens are set aside approximately one hour before retiring to bed. | Their light and excitement render sleep harder to attain | Screens consume too much electricity at night | Screens cease to function late in the evening | Their light causes the eyes to grow tired more quickly
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises a well-composed plate of food? | Half vegetables and fruit, a quarter grains, a quarter protein | Half protein, a quarter sweets, a quarter grains | Entirely grains, with fruit served only at weekends | Half sweets, with the remainder vegetables
exam: Ascertain the quantity of sleep required nightly by a child of nine to ten years. | Approximately nine to twelve hours | Approximately four to six hours | Approximately fifteen to eighteen hours | Approximately six to seven hours
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning raw eggs, raw flour and uncooked meat. | They may carry germs and are eaten only when properly cooked | They are safe provided they have been refrigerated | They are safer than cooked food, as nothing is destroyed | They are harmful only when eaten in the evening
